Define $Phi: GL_2(CC) -> cal(M)$ by $mat(a, b; c, d) |-> (z |-> (a z + b) / (c z + d))$. The multiplication calculation for $cal(M)$ makes $Phi$ a homomorphism, and every Möbius transformation has a representing matrix in $GL_2(CC)$, so $Phi$ is surjective.

A matrix $mat(a, b; c, d)$ lies in $ker Phi$ exactly when its image fixes $0$, $1$, and $oo$. The three-point lemma gives $b = 0$, $c = 0$, and $a = d$. Thus $ker Phi$ is the subgroup of scalar matrices identified with $CC^times$. The isomorphism theorem then gives $GL_2(CC) / CC^times teq cal(M)$.
